deep.reconstruction:
Context: notes of July 1986 on a combinatorial-logical structure that Grothendieck first called a 'magasin' and here renames 'atelier'. In the previous pages, a 'déploiement' X of M is an element of P(P(L)), a multi-structure is an element of P(P(P(L))), and 'figures' F are subsets of a base set M, with an incidence relation Δ.

This page does two things.

(1) Terminology. The words 'atelier' and 'magasin' are swapped: 'atelier' now names what was called 'magasin', and conversely. The axioms Mag 1-Mag 4 are renamed At 1-At 4.

(2) Supports. He returns to the 'supports' ('contours') attached to an atelier. The underlying set is now written 𝒜 rather than M, so the structure is a quadruple (𝒜, ⊴, ≪°, |∘|):
- ⊴ is an order-type relation;
- ≪° is a second relation, a sort of strict or 'well-inside' relation;
- |∘| is a symmetric, irreflexive relation, i.e. an orthogonality or 'disjointness' relation.

From |∘| he defines a family Σ_𝒜 ⊂ P(𝒜) of distinguished subsets, the 'supports'. These are closed under ≪° but in general not under ⊴, and the family is stable under arbitrary intersections. Σ_𝒜 is thus a Moore (closure) family, hence a complete lattice in which the infima are intersections.

The next page completes the picture:
- Σ contains ∅ (least element) and E (greatest element);
- complementation ∁ is an anti-involution (order-reversing and involutive), which yields arbitrary suprema and exchanges Inf and Sup;
- |∘| extends to supports by S|∘|S' iff x|∘|x' for all x in S and x' in S';
- S|∘|S' ⟺ S ⊂ ∁S' ⟺ S' ⊂ ∁S, and S|∘|S' implies S ∩ S' = ∅ but not conversely.

In modern terms this is the classical construction of the complete ortholattice of biorthogonally closed sets S = S^⊥⊥ of an orthogonality space (X, ⊥), with ∁S = S^⊥. It is a Galois connection / polarity of a symmetric relation, as in Birkhoff 1940; orthogonality spaces were studied by Dacey (1968) and Foulis-Randall. The marginal note on the next page ('Structure d'un Spatios': order + ∁ with least/greatest element, anti-involution, x∧∁x = 0, x∨∁x = 1) states exactly the ortholattice axioms.

The purpose seems to be to give an atelier a 'space-like' lattice of supports or contours. Constructible parts, coming from figures and an incidence relation, will then be singled out inside it. The extra condition of closure under ≪° is non-standard and depends on the undefined axioms At 1-At 4.
